---
import InlineCommand from "../../components/InlineCommand.tsx";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import PromptComparison from "../../components/PromptComparison.tsx";
import TerminalBlock from "../../components/TerminalBlock.tsx";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Warm-up Task - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="warm-up-task">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-800 mb-4">App Overview: The Dashboard</h2>
        <p>
          For these exercises, you'll be working on a <strong>Productivity Dashboard</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dashboard is built using <strong>React</strong>, <strong>TypeScript</strong>, and <strong>Tailwind CSS</strong>, with <strong
            >TanStack Router</strong
          > handling navigation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>Current Feature Status</h3>
        <p>
          The application is currently in a partially built state. Some core infrastructure is in place, while several key features are waiting for your
          implementation.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
          <div class="bg-teal-50 border border-teal-200 rounded-lg p-5">
            <h4 class="text-teal-800 font-bold mt-0 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-500"></span>
              Implemented
            </h4>
            <ul class="space-y-2 mb-0 list-none pl-0">
              <li class="text-sm text-teal-900"><strong>Dashboard Shell:</strong> The main layout, navigation, and theme toggling.</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-teal-900"><strong>Important Dates:</strong> A simple list for tracking upcoming deadlines.</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-teal-900"><strong>Todo List:</strong> Basic CRUD functionality for daily tasks.</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-teal-900"><strong>World Clock:</strong> Displays current time across multiple time zones.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-5">
            <h4 class="text-amber-800 font-bold mt-0 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-amber-500"></span>
              Pending (Your Tasks)
            </h4>
            <ul class="space-y-2 mb-0 list-none pl-0">
              <li class="text-sm text-amber-900"><strong>Notepad:</strong> A dashboard widget for quick scratchpad notes.</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-amber-900"><strong>Pomodoro Timer:</strong> A focus timer tool with preset intervals.</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-amber-900"><strong>Habit Tracker:</strong> A visual grid for tracking weekly streaks.</li>
              <li class="text-sm text-amber-900">And finally, a feature of your choice.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="bg-sky-50 border border-sky-200 rounded-lg px-6 py-5 my-8">
        <h4 class="font-bold mt-0 mb-2 text-sky-600">Remember the Basics</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-sky-800 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          As you move into these tasks, keep the <a href="/fundamentals/prompt-engineering">Prompt Engineering Basics</a> in mind. These will save you from endless
          back-and-forth debugging:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 mb-0">
          <li class="text-sky-900 text-sm">
            <strong>Explain the 'Why':</strong> Don't just ask for a feature; explain what user problem it solves. Context prevents hallucinations.
          </li>
          <li class="text-sky-900 text-sm">
            <strong>Bite-Sized Tasks:</strong> AI performs best on scoped, specific requests. Don't build the whole "Notepad" in one prompt; move task by task.
          </li>
          <li class="text-sky-900 text-sm">
            <strong>Use Golden Masters:</strong> When asking the AI to write a new component, reference an existing component or feature as a stylistic example.
          </li>
          <li class="text-sky-900 text-sm">
            <strong>Reference Your Code:</strong> Explicitly mention filenames like <code>@src/lib/storage.ts</code> so the AI knows which existing utilities to reuse.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <section>
        <h3>Workflow & CLI Commands</h3>
        <p>
          Note that throughout these tasks, you'll need to master going back and forth between prompting your AI assistant and <a
            href="/development/utilizing-git">Utilizing Git</a
          > to keep track of changes and revert where necessary.
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-800 mt-0 mb-2 text-sm">1. Execution</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-3">
              Once you have run <InlineCommand command="gemini" client:visible /> and authenticated with your Google account, you may initiate prompts in your terminal.{" "}
              <strong>Remember:</strong> Anytime you believe your context has been compressed, or gemini is hanging, commit what you have and start a new conversation.
            </p>
            <TerminalBlock
              title="terminal"
              lines={[
                { type: "comment", text: "# Kill the current process with Ctrl + C" },
                { type: "command", command: "^C" },
                { type: "comment", text: "# Relaunch" },
                { type: "command", command: "gemini" },
              ]}
              client:visible
            />
          </div>

          <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-800 mt-0 mb-2 text-sm">2. Git Workflow (via git & gh)</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-3">
              Use standard Git commands to manage the AI's changes. The goal is to commit after <strong>every successful prompt cycle</strong>. We use <InlineCommand
                command="gh"
                client:visible
              /> for repository and PR management.
            </p>
            <div class="space-y-3">
              <TerminalBlock
                title="commit changes"
                lines={[
                  { type: "comment", text: "# Commit after a successful prompt" },
                  { type: "command", command: 'git add . && git commit -m "fix: resolve world clock bug"' },
                ]}
                client:visible
              />
              <TerminalBlock
                title="revert changes"
                lines={[
                  { type: "comment", text: "# Revert to your last good state" },
                  { type: "command", command: "git reset --hard HEAD" },
                  { type: "comment", text: "# OR" },
                  { type: "command", command: "git checkout ." },
                ]}
                client:visible
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-stone-100 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-6">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">A Note on Other Tools</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-800 leading-relaxed mb-0">
            While these exercises use Gemini CLI, the principles remain identical in <strong>Claude Code</strong>, <strong>Cursor</strong>, or <strong
              >GitHub Copilot</strong
            >.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>Task: UI Bug Audit</h3>
        <p>
          Before building new features, we need to address some bugs in the existing UI. Navigate through the dashboard and identify three specific UI bugs that
          have been intentionally left in the codebase.
        </p>

        <details class="group bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-6">
          <summary class="list-none cursor-pointer flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-stone-700">
            <span>Need a hint?</span>
            <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▼</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-stone-600 border-t border-stone-200 pt-4">
            <p class="text-sm"><strong>Hint 1:</strong> Try adding a new important date. Look at the "Category" dropdown's icon spacing.</p>
            <p class="text-sm">
              <strong>Hint 2:</strong> Check the "World Clock" widget on the dashboard. Are the clock numbers fitting properly in the cards?
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm"><strong>Hint 3:</strong> Navigate to any page and look at the bottom of the page title. Does the title baseline look correct?</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>Exercise: The Prompt Comparison Test</h3>
        <p>
          Now that we know what's broken, the goal is to craft a prompt that works. How you structure your request directly determines how long you will spend
          fixing this bug.
        </p>
        <p>
          To better understand this, execute the following two prompts against the <strong>World Clock</strong> overflow bug. Between each prompt revert
          your codebase to its original state so you can compare them.
        </p>

        <PromptComparison client:visible />

        <div class="space-y-4 mb-8 mt-4">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-800 text-md italic">Execution Steps:</h4>
          <ol class="text-sm text-stone-700 space-y-3">
            <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Run <strong>Prompt #1</strong> in the Gemini CLI. Observe how the AI responds and what changes it makes as well as how long it takes.</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Step 2:</strong> Revert the changes using <InlineCommand command="git reset --hard HEAD" client:visible /> (or <InlineCommand
                command="git checkout ."
                client:visible
              />) to return to a clean state.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Step 3:</strong> Run <strong>Prompt #2</strong>. Observe the difference in response quality, accuracy, and how much guessing the AI does.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <div class="bg-stone-100 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-6">
            <h5 class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-600 uppercase tracking-wider mt-0 mb-2">Referencing Files in Other Tools</h5>
            <p class="text-sm text-stone-800 leading-relaxed mb-0">
              If you're using most tools, you can "attach" files to your prompt by typing <code>@</code> followed by the filename (e.g., <code
                >@WorldClockCard.tsx</code
              >).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Notice the vast difference in output quality between the two prompts, both in interaction with you, the decision maker, as well as in the actual
          result.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Prompt #1</strong> is a <i>passive</i> prompt. By providing no context and no direction, you force the AI to guess. It will likely spend several
          turns trying to find the file, misinterpreting "fitting" as a logic error rather than a CSS overflow, and producing a generic "fix" that might break other
          components. On edit, it does not ask you any clarifying questions before proceeding with making changes that will likely ultimately fail to fix the bug.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Prompt #2</strong> is <i>assertive and diagnostic</i>. You identified the root cause, pointed to the exact files, and set clear constraints.
          By providing the file path you're restricting the AI's search space, which significantly reduces the risk of it hallucinating a fix in an unrelated
          CSS file. By asking for options instead of a blind fix, and by forcing the AI to ask clarifying questions before proceeding with edits, you maintained
          strong ownership of the code and ultimately had better understanding and control over the output. The result is almost always a higher-quality fix on
          the first try.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-6">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">Tip: Built-in Undo</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-800 leading-relaxed mb-0">
            While <InlineCommand command="git reset" client:visible /> is the safest way to revert your entire workspace, you can also use the <InlineCommand
              command="/rewind"
              client:visible
            /> command directly in the Gemini CLI. This will step backward through your conversation history and automatically undo any file edits made by the AI
            in those turns. You can also type <InlineCommand command="/" client:visible /> in the CLI terminal at any time to see a full list of slash commands and
            customization options.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h3>Discovery & Documentation</h3>
        <p>Before you continue to fix the rest of the bugs, feel free to use the AI to help you understand the rest of the codebase.</p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
          <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-700 mt-0 mb-2 text-sm italic">"Ask" Mode</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-0 leading-relaxed">
              Don't jump straight to edits. Use the AI to ask architectural questions: <em>"How does the useStorage hook work?"</em> or <em
                >"What is the layout pattern for existing cards?"</em
              >
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5">
            <h4 class="font-bold text-stone-700 mt-0 mb-2 text-sm italic">Review AGENTS.md</h4>
            <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-0 leading-relaxed">
              Refer back to <a href="/task-breakdowns/agents-md">this lesson</a> and review <code>AGENTS.md</code> in the project root. It provides the AI with its
              primary context. Update it with any new technical details you discover during your debugging sessions.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-stone-200 pt-8">
        <p class="text-stone-800 font-medium">
          Now that you've set up your environment and experimented with prompts, take some time to fix the remaining UI bugs. Experiment with prompts: use
          markdown in prompts, provide bulleted instructions, have it ask you questions, have it provide options, etc. to determine what works best for you.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-800 font-medium">
          Once you've committed those fixes, you're ready to move on to the next lesson where we'll start building features from scratch.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
